title: _request cannot emit a repeated query parameter
updated: 2026-08-27T06:35:46Z
---

_request's params encoder walks `sort keys %{$opts{params}}` and pushes exactly
one `k=v` pair per key. A HashRef value is JSON-encoded; an ArrayRef value is
not handled at all and stringifies to ARRAY(0x...). There is no way to ask for
`names=a&names=b`.

That shape is not optional for GET /images/get. Measured against the rootless
Podman socket (5.4.2, API 1.41):

  ?names=alpine%3A3&names=registry%3A2   -> 200, both images in manifest.json
  ?names=alpine%3A3%2Cregistry%3A2       -> 500, {"message":"normalizing image:
                                            normalizing name for compat API:
                                            parsing reference
                                            \"alpine:3,registry:2\": invalid
                                            reference format"}
  (no names at all)                      -> 400, {"message":"no images to
                                            download"}

Docker reads r.Form["names"], so a single comma-joined value is one literal
image name there too. Repeated pairs are the only spelling either engine
accepts.

images->get_all (karr #20) therefore builds its query string by hand and hands
it over as part of the path, with a private _names_query in
API::Docker::API::Images that mirrors Role::HTTP::_uri_encode's rule (`/` and
`:` left raw so an image reference survives). That is a workaround, not a
design: the encoding now lives in two places and can drift.

The fix: teach _request's params loop to expand an ArrayRef value into one
pair per element. Then get_all becomes

    $self->client->get('/images/get', params => { names => \@names }, raw => 1)

and _names_query is deleted along with its comment block. t/images_tar.t's
'get_all: names is a repeated query parameter' and 'get_all: the escaping keeps
an image reference intact' subtests assert the wire form, not the mechanism, so
they carry over unchanged.

POST /commit's `changes` is the other multi-value parameter in the API, but it
does not need this: measured on the same engine, one newline-joined value and
two repeated pairs produce the same image, because the engine parses each value
as a Dockerfile snippet and a snippet may span lines. images->commit sends the
joined form.
